# Contributing to CAGT

CAGT welcomes contributions from anyone, provided they fall within a
reasonable scope aligned with the project's goals: a single-file AMD GPU
fan controller with a ncurses TUI and optional daemon mode, with zero
external dependencies beyond standard C and ncurses.

## Project Philosophy

CAGT is intentionally a **single .c file**. Before contributing, consider
whether your change respects this constraint. Features that require
additional source files, build systems, or external libraries (beyond
ncurses) will generally be declined. The tool should remain:

- Compilable with a single `gcc` command
- Runnable from any terminal
- Safe on exit (always restores automatic fan control)

## Testing

Testing the software in good faith, on real AMD hardware, is strongly
encouraged. Testers help improve reliability across different GPU
generations and kernel versions. No warranty is provided, and testers
assume all risks; the license's disclaimer of liability applies in full.

At minimum, contributors should verify:

- GPU detection works on their hardware
- Temperature reads correctly
- Fan speed changes take effect
- Exiting (Q, Ctrl+C, SIGTERM) restores auto fan control
- Manual mode toggles and adjustments behave as expected
- Daemon mode starts and responds to CLI commands
- Logging works when enabled

## Pull Requests and Code Contributions

All contributions (pull requests, patches, suggestions) are reviewed
carefully before acceptance. By submitting code, you confirm that you have
the right to contribute it under the project's license.

Before submitting, contributors are expected to have tested their changes
locally on real AMD hardware. Untested or poorly verified changes may be
rejected or delayed pending further validation.

These testing requirements apply to external contributors. The project
maintainer reserves the right to commit changes directly to the repository
without prior public testing or review. All such commits remain subject to
the license's disclaimer of liability.

Malicious or intentionally harmful contributions will be rejected and the
submitter permanently banned from further participation. This includes, but
is not limited to, introducing backdoors, spyware, or any code intended to
damage hardware (e.g., setting dangerous PWM values or disabling thermal
safety mechanisms).

## Credit

Contributors whose code is merged into the main repository will be credited
in the project's README.md, listing their name alongside the release
version(s) their work appears in, unless they request otherwise. Credit is
given as a sign of appreciation and does not imply any additional rights or
responsibilities.

## Code of Conduct

Be respectful, constructive, and honest. Treat others as you would in a
professional engineering team. Harassment, spam, or disruptive behaviour
will not be tolerated.